minimization of a locally convex functional on an abstract (real) Hilbert space \(X\). Let \(\|\,.\,\|_{X}\) and \((.,.)_{X}\) denote respectively the norm and associated scalar product of \(X\) and let \(J\) be a \(\mathcal{C}^{2}\)-mapping from \(X\) into \(\mathbb{R}\). We propose to minimize \(J\) over an adequate subset \(D\) of \(X\), chosen as follows. First, since we are only interested in a minimum of \(J\) we restrict the discussion to the set

\[\{v\in X;\,J(v)\leq C_{0}\}\]

for some constant \(C_{0}\); of course, we suppose that this set is not empty and we take for \(D\) one of its connected components. In other words, \(D\) is a non empty connected component of:

\[\{v\in X;\,J(v)\leq C_{0}\}.\] (6.1)

Now, we assume that the functional \(J\) is _strictly convex in \(D\)_, namely:

there exist two constants \(\alpha>0\) and \(M>0\) such that all \(v\) in \(D\) satisfy

\[\alpha\|\,w\,\|_{X}^{2}\leq D^{2}J(v)\cdot(w,w)\leq M\|\,w\|_{X}^{2}\ \ \ \ \ \forall w\in X.\] (6.2)

Then it is well known that the problem

\[\inf_{v\in D}J(v)\] (6.3)

has a unique solution \(u\) in \(D\) characterized by

\[DJ(u)=0.\] (6.4)

As usual, we associate with \(DJ\) the gradient \(g\) defined by:

\[(g(v),w)_{X}=\langle DJ(v),w\rangle\ \ \ \ \ \forall w\in X,\]

where \(\langle.,.\rangle\) denotes the duality pairing \(X\) and \(X^{\prime}\).

To solve Problem (6.3) we introduce the following general method of descent _starting from \(u^{0}\in D\)_:

\[\left\{\begin{aligned} & 1^{\circ}\text{\rm for all }m\geq 0,\text{\rm choose a direction of descent }w^{m}\in X\text{\rm and define }\rho^{m}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}\text{\rm by }\\ &\left\{\begin{aligned} & (6.5)& J(u^{m}-\rho^{m}w^{m})=\inf_{ \rho\geq 0\atop u^{m}-\rho w^{m}\in D}J(u^{m}-\rho w^{m}),\\ & 2^{\circ}\text{\rm set }\\ & u^{m+1}=u^{m}-\rho^{m}w^{m}\in D.\end{aligned}\right.\] (6.6)

As shown below, this algorithm converges under reasonable assumptions.

**Theorem 6.1**.: _Let \(D\) be a non empty, connected subset of \(X\) satisfying (6.1) and let \(J\) satisfy (6.2) in \(D\). If, for each \(m\), the direction of descent \(w^{m}\in X\) is such that_

\[(g(u^{m}),w^{m})_{X}\geq\beta\|\,g(u^{m})\|_{X}\|\,w^{m}\|_{X}\] (6.7)

_for some constant \(\beta>0\) independent of \(m\)_, _then the algorithm of descent (6.5) (6.6) defines a sequence \((u^{m})\in D\) that converges to the unique solution \(u\) of (6.3)._
